===Etymology 1===
{{etyl|enm}} ''[[relai#Middle English|relai]]'' "fresh team of dogs for a hunt" (cf {{enm}} ''[[relaien#Middle English|relaien]]'' "to relay") from {{etyl|frm}} ''[[relai#Middle French|relai]]'' "reserve pack of hounds" from ''[[relaier#Middle French|relaier]]'' "to exchange tired animals for fresh", lit. "to leave behind" from {{etyl|fro}} ''[[relaier#Old French|relaier]]'' "to leave behind" from ''[[re-#Old French|re-]]'' + ''[[laier#Old French|laier]]'' "to leave", of {{etyl|gem}} origin--a conflation of {{etyl|frk}} *''[[latjan#Frankish|latjan]]'' "to delay, hinder" (from {{proto|Germanic|latjan|to delay, hinder, stall|lang=}} from {{proto|Indo-European|le(i)d-|to leave, leave behind|lang=}}) and {{etyl|frk}} *''[[laibjan#Frankish|laibjan]]'' "to leave" (from {{proto|Germanic|laibijan-, laibián-|to leave, cause to stay|lang=}} from {{proto|Indo-European|leip-|to remain, continue|lang=}}). Akin to {{etyl|ang|-}} ''[[latian#Old English|latian]]'' "to delay, hesitate" (from {{ang}} ''[[latu#Old English|latu]]'' "a delay, a hindrance"), {{ang}} ''[[læfan#Old English|lǣfan]]'' "to leave". More at [[let]], [[leave]]

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|/ˈriːleɪ/}}
* {{rhymes|iːleɪ}}
* Homophones: [[re-lay]]

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{electronics}} An electrical [[actuator]] that allows a relatively small electrical [[voltage]] or [[current]] to control a larger voltage or current.
# {{context|athletics}} A [[track and field]] discipline where runners take turns in carrying a [[baton]] from start to finish. Most common events are 4x100 meter and 4x400 meter competitions.

